The HuggingFace incident of July 2026 was one of the first large-scale operations of misaligned agents. While frontier labs have endeavoured to ensure that their agents behave in an acceptable manner, there have been numerous instances of failure. When granted internet access (or have otherwise been able to exploit their way there), models have been reported to attempt blackmail [1], upload malicious code to package registries [2], source stolen credentials [3] and collude with other agents [4]. 


Notably, OpenAI's recommendations after the HuggingFace incident pointed in the direction of the breached company, leaving the more more pertinent question of how to detect and prevent misaligned model behaviour in the first place wide open. This demonstrates the need for auditors to be empowered with an agentless framework to detect misaligned models deployed by organisations. The response to these incidents have shown that responsibility and asuurance of AI safety cannot be delegated to individual organisations through a standard involving asuurance provided by third party regulators.

Our framework, CanaryNet, sits at the intersection of the two solution spaces: deception to detect misaligned behaviour, _before_ it causes significant harm. It is tailored to the increasing regulatory scrutiny large AI labs are facing - providing evidence of aligned model behaviour, but without needing access to the labs' internal systems. We have repurposed the existing cybersecurity concept of IoCs, indicators of compromise, into something ready for the AI safety era: indicators of misalignment (IoMs). Our framework determines the actions that misaligned agents may take in the pursuit of a given task, and places voluminous canary resources across the internet to detect them. This includes resources observed in recent breach reports, alongside some novel exploit paths: including GitHub repositories, websites, personal blogs, servers, message boards, and "personalities" designed to detect agentic manipulation. We further prove that if deplo

